(6197289, 913804, 113419, '<td valign="top">making another comment here cause your photo is stuck in my critique que.<br/> <br/>Said it before and I''ll say it again, I like this photo</td>', 'making another comment here cause your photo is stuck in my critique que. Said it before and I''ll say it again, I like this photo', '2010-10-21 00:05:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(6197632, 913889, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Chris - here we are again!<br/> <br/>This will probably be a much shorter critique, since there really isn''t much to say that hasn''t already been said. It is a pleasant shot - but unremarkable. You said that it came from trialling a technique, and that is pretty much what it looks like. That said, from where I''m sitting you <i>have </i>nailed the fill flash thing.<br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>: It''s all good - the focus, the DOF and especially the light. You have managed to get a nicely exposed background while capturing all of the hummingbird detail courtesy of the flash. <br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>:  I think the biggest issue is that tiny subject in that fairly open frame. A busier and more interesting background could bolster a composition like this - setting the subject in its environment, but there just isn''t enough there to do this.  Another minor point (which I got from a pro wildlife photographer''s book) is about the angle of shot. Here you are looking up at the bird, which is the angle most people also have so it doesn''t really grab them as being different. It may not be possible, but a horizontal or downward angle might add interest. Equally, apparently we should try and shoot land animals horizontal or upwards... got to be worth a try!<br/> <br/>In summary, it is technically spot on and you definitely achieved what you were going for, so I guess now the mission is to park that little bit closer!! Happy hunting.<br/> <br/>Frank.</td>', 'Hi Chris - here we are again! This will probably be a much shorter critique, since there really isn''t much to say that hasn''t already been said. It is a pleasant shot - but unremarkable. You said that it came from trialling a technique, and that is pretty much what it looks like. That said, from where I''m sitting you have nailed the fill flash thing. Technically: It''s all good - the focus, the DOF and especially the light. You have managed to get a nicely exposed background while capturing all of the hummingbird detail courtesy of the flash.  Artistically:  I think the biggest issue is that tiny subject in that fairly open frame. A busier and more interesting background could bolster a composition like this - setting the subject in its environment, but there just isn''t enough there to do this.  Another minor point (which I got from a pro wildlife photographer''s book) is about the angle of shot. Here you are looking up at the bird, which is the angle most people also have so it doesn''t really grab them as being different. It may not be possible, but a horizontal or downward angle might add interest. Equally, apparently we should try and shoot land animals horizontal or upwards... got to be worth a try! In summary, it is technically spot on and you definitely achieved what you were going for, so I guess now the mission is to park that little bit closer!! Happy hunting. Frank.', '2010-10-21 12:55:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(6197749, 913868, 113419, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings From the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>wow!  Great photo!  love the pov<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>you got this at a good time of day for the back lighting and you did a nice job with the fill flash; wonderful composition; I think the boarder is very well done<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>you got it <br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>I''m not sure, but I think it would take a horse person to really connect with this photo.  Or maybe just a horse lover.  (I myself am a former horser person and still a horse lover) <br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>I love the pov on this picture but it maybe where some of your lower votes came from.  It could be a not so flattering angle to some folks.  I also see that folks commented on the noisy sky.  Yeah, that could be an overall problem, but I really don''t see the sky as I''m looking at the horse.  The best suggestion I have would be a little added light towards the forhead and ears.</td>', 'Greetings From the Critique Club  First Impressions: wow!  Great photo!  love the pov Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): you got this at a good time of day for the back lighting and you did a nice job with the fill flash; wonderful composition; I think the boarder is very well done Challenge: you got it  Artistic/Emotion: I''m not sure, but I think it would take a horse person to really connect with this photo.  Or maybe just a horse lover.  (I myself am a former horser person and still a horse lover)  Summary: I love the pov on this picture but it maybe where some of your lower votes came from.  It could be a not so flattering angle to some folks.  I also see that folks commented on the noisy sky.  Yeah, that could be an overall problem, but I really don''t see the sky as I''m looking at the horse.  The best suggestion I have would be a little added light towards the forhead and ears.', '2010-10-21 15:41:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198155, 913220, 28359, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-) <br/> <br/>Congrats on your top ten finish.  I must agree with some of the comments below. I gave your image a 9. It should had finished higher. Your control of the fill flash is excellent. I''m one who likes to see a scene in a image. The background is awesome. The composition is excellent. The sepia tones work real well. If there was one thing I would nick pick on, I would like to see a little more light in the eyes. Excellent work!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-)  Congrats on your top ten finish.  I must agree with some of the comments below. I gave your image a 9. It should had finished higher. Your control of the fill flash is excellent. I''m one who likes to see a scene in a image. The background is awesome. The composition is excellent. The sepia tones work real well. If there was one thing I would nick pick on, I would like to see a little more light in the eyes. Excellent work! ', '2010-10-22 01:59:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198171, 913854, 28359, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-) <br/> <br/>I really like this shot. The warm color is very soothing. Has good clarity with movement for a abstract type of image. I think the composition could had been a little better by moving the center apple to the left some to show the highlights to shadows from 1/3 to 1/3 to 1/3 if that makes any sense. I had little trouble tying the image to the challenge with the shadow in front. I didn''t get the sense of fill flash. Pretty cool image.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-)  I really like this shot. The warm color is very soothing. Has good clarity with movement for a abstract type of image. I think the composition could had been a little better by moving the center apple to the left some to show the highlights to shadows from 1/3 to 1/3 to 1/3 if that makes any sense. I had little trouble tying the image to the challenge with the shadow in front. I didn''t get the sense of fill flash. Pretty cool image.', '2010-10-22 02:19:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198185, 913133, 28359, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-) <br/> <br/>What a lovely young lady. The angle and composition is excellent. It meets the challenge requirements well, the lighting is great. I think you nailed the main reason why the image didn''t score higher, soft focus is OK in a portrait but the eyes are the key. They need to be sharp imo to draw you into the face. I have mixed feelings on the processing. I like it, yet I think you could had backed off just a little. The over all tones/colors would had worked better for me. It does have a artsy feel to it.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-)  What a lovely young lady. The angle and composition is excellent. It meets the challenge requirements well, the lighting is great. I think you nailed the main reason why the image didn''t score higher, soft focus is OK in a portrait but the eyes are the key. They need to be sharp imo to draw you into the face. I have mixed feelings on the processing. I like it, yet I think you could had backed off just a little. The over all tones/colors would had worked better for me. It does have a artsy feel to it.', '2010-10-22 02:42:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198225, 913797, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Robert, welcome to the critique zone! <br/> <br/>My initial take on this shot is that it has a really warm, rich feel - like a proper chesterfield sofa. The soft reds and browns work really well with the rumpled fur to give an impression of comfort and tactile pleasures.<br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>:  Your focus, and control of the DOF is unsurprisingly superb - the OOF cushion on the right hand side works nicely to isolate Brittany''s expression. The only area where I would take some issue is lighting, partly because I would have liked just a little more detail in the left eye, and partly because I am not convinced that this is ''fill flash'' rather than just ''flash''. That seems pretty contentious at the moment!<br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>: This is completely subjective, but I find the fairly central position of the face a little odd. I can''t see what the space to the right is adding - a little is good for the isolation effect, but there is a lot there. That''s all though, as I said before the ''feel'' of the photo, the colours and textures, are just great.<br/> <br/>All in all, a really classic study of Brittany - I''m sure she would be pleased! <br/> <br/>Happy hunting.<br/> <br/>Frank.</td>', 'Hi Robert, welcome to the critique zone!  My initial take on this shot is that it has a really warm, rich feel - like a proper chesterfield sofa. The soft reds and browns work really well with the rumpled fur to give an impression of comfort and tactile pleasures. Technically:  Your focus, and control of the DOF is unsurprisingly superb - the OOF cushion on the right hand side works nicely to isolate Brittany''s expression. The only area where I would take some issue is lighting, partly because I would have liked just a little more detail in the left eye, and partly because I am not convinced that this is ''fill flash'' rather than just ''flash''. That seems pretty contentious at the moment! Artistically: This is completely subjective, but I find the fairly central position of the face a little odd. I can''t see what the space to the right is adding - a little is good for the isolation effect, but there is a lot there. That''s all though, as I said before the ''feel'' of the photo, the colours and textures, are just great. All in all, a really classic study of Brittany - I''m sure she would be pleased!  Happy hunting. Frank.', '2010-10-22 05:14:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198228, 913875, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Shailesh, welcome to the critique zone! It''s always hard to critique a ribbon winner so this might be quite short!<br/> <br/>My initial take is that this is a very bright, cheerful image of an excellent model. As another commenter said, it does look like a professional portrait shot - but with soul.<br/> <br/>Technically, there is nothing I can really say about this. The focus and DOF control are bang on, as one would expect from your work. There is the raging question about ''fill flash'' but I am sure you are sick of that one and since the voters have spoken, who am I to disagree!<br/> <br/>Artistically, the composition is simple and appealing. Kudos to your son for pulling off such a great expression - excitement and fun, with just a touch of uncertainty/shyness lingering in the eyes. I''m not sure about the use of such an extreme angle of tilt, but definitely some tilt does give it interest.<br/> <br/>All in all, a cracking shot and recognised as such by the voters.</td>', 'Hi Shailesh, welcome to the critique zone! It''s always hard to critique a ribbon winner so this might be quite short! My initial take is that this is a very bright, cheerful image of an excellent model. As another commenter said, it does look like a professional portrait shot - but with soul. Technically, there is nothing I can really say about this. The focus and DOF control are bang on, as one would expect from your work. There is the raging question about ''fill flash'' but I am sure you are sick of that one and since the voters have spoken, who am I to disagree! Artistically, the composition is simple and appealing. Kudos to your son for pulling off such a great expression - excitement and fun, with just a touch of uncertainty/shyness lingering in the eyes. I''m not sure about the use of such an extreme angle of tilt, but definitely some tilt does give it interest. All in all, a cracking shot and recognised as such by the voters.', '2010-10-22 05:21:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198230, 913692, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Stan, welcome to the Critique Zone!<br/> <br/>I''m afraid I have to be honest here, my first take on this shot is that it looks like a snap of a friend at a BBQ, so what went wrong? <br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>:  The shot is just not that sharp. I can see some sharpening has been done to rescue it, but (having tried similar myself) it never really works. I would suggest closing the aperture down - the kit lenses are never that great wide open. Boost your ISO to 200 or 400 and close your aperture to f6.3 or f7.1, make sure that the focus is bang on where you want it (the face, not the trousers!) and good to go. The lighting is more ''flash'' than ''fill flash'' too - the darkness behind your subject makes true ''fill flash'' unnecessary.<br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>:  You have carefully used the rule of thirds, but the slightly dull background doesn''t add any interest - it''s dark and fairly homogenous. I would either go in much tighter, or shoot against a more interesting background. The point of view is also a problem here - this is the same point of view that we would see in normal life, and as such we don''t tend to be grabbed by it - try getting low and shooting up, or shooting from above for a more unusual perspective.<br/> <br/><b>PP</b>: You don''t mention saturating, but these colours (particularly the blue) are way over-saturated. I find de-sat can be useful on these occasions. Also be very wary of the over-sharpening. Some things just can''t be recovered.<br/> <br/>It is clear from your portfolio that you are way better than this shot, which is perhaps really just an exercise in lighting, so I don''t want to get you down! Just head out and get back shooting! Happy hunting,<br/> <br/>Frank.</td>', 'Hi Stan, welcome to the Critique Zone! I''m afraid I have to be honest here, my first take on this shot is that it looks like a snap of a friend at a BBQ, so what went wrong?  Technically:  The shot is just not that sharp. I can see some sharpening has been done to rescue it, but (having tried similar myself) it never really works. I would suggest closing the aperture down - the kit lenses are never that great wide open. Boost your ISO to 200 or 400 and close your aperture to f6.3 or f7.1, make sure that the focus is bang on where you want it (the face, not the trousers!) and good to go. The lighting is more ''flash'' than ''fill flash'' too - the darkness behind your subject makes true ''fill flash'' unnecessary. Artistically:  You have carefully used the rule of thirds, but the slightly dull background doesn''t add any interest - it''s dark and fairly homogenous. I would either go in much tighter, or shoot against a more interesting background. The point of view is also a problem here - this is the same point of view that we would see in normal life, and as such we don''t tend to be grabbed by it - try getting low and shooting up, or shooting from above for a more unusual perspective. PP: You don''t mention saturating, but these colours (particularly the blue) are way over-saturated. I find de-sat can be useful on these occasions. Also be very wary of the over-sharpening. Some things just can''t be recovered. It is clear from your portfolio that you are way better than this shot, which is perhaps really just an exercise in lighting, so I don''t want to get you down! Just head out and get back shooting! Happy hunting, Frank.', '2010-10-22 05:32:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(6198252, 913854, 119419, '<td valign="top">Unfortunately I only gave this a 7 in voting, on review, I think it is a stunning image and the poem is so fitting. I''m now giving you an extra 3 points that you deserve.</td>', 'Unfortunately I only gave this a 7 in voting, on review, I think it is a stunning image and the poem is so fitting. I''m now giving you an extra 3 points that you deserve.', '2010-10-22 08:56:41.000000', NULL, 0),
